# Committed readings for p3exit0.wasm and p3panic.wasm, the fork's first GOOS=wasip3 components (#1236, F1).
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run <case>.wasm   (no -W flag: the memory is unshared)
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64, 2026-11-14.
#
# reader                                      reading
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3exit0, 10 of 10        exit 0
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3panic, 10 of 10        trap unreachable
# Burroughs at d32b0eb on p3exit0, 10 of 10   exit 0
# Burroughs at d32b0eb on p3panic, 10 of 10   trap unreachable
#
# Meaning:     "exit N" is the process's exit code with a run that ended by wasi:cli/exit@0.3.0's exit;
#              "trap R" is a trap whose kind is R. Neither case wrote a byte to stdout or stderr from the
#              guest: F1 lowers no stdout, so p3panic's message is never printed.
# Burroughs:   `burroughs run <case>.wasm`, a binary built from cmd/burroughs at d32b0eb: p3exit0 exited 0;
#              p3panic exited 4 and printed "burroughs: trap: unreachable" to stderr, 10 of 10 each.
#
# wasmtime's p3panic stderr, identical in all 10 runs (sha256 of each run's stderr compared). The innermost Go
# frame names the refusal: usleep, called by freezetheworld before the panic's first write, which is why the
# trap is usleep's and not write1's.
#
#   Error: failed to run main module `p3panic.wasm`
#
#   Caused by:
#       0: error while executing at wasm backtrace:
#       0: 0x113481 - <unknown>!runtime.abort
#       1:  0x8c788 - <unknown>!runtime.usleep
#       2:  0x9b84a - <unknown>!runtime.freezetheworld
#       3:  0x91743 - <unknown>!runtime.startpanic_m
#       4: 0x101059 - <unknown>!runtime.fatalpanic.func1
#       5: 0x113424 - <unknown>!runtime.systemstack
#       6:  0x91208 - <unknown>!runtime.fatalpanic
#       7: 0x10c157 - <unknown>!runtime.gopanic
#       8: 0x11565a - <unknown>!main.main
#       9:  0x96d10 - <unknown>!runtime.main
#      10: 0x113767 - <unknown>!wasm_pc_f_loop
#      11: 0x113822 - <unknown>!_rt0_wasm_wasip3
#       1: wasm trap: wasm `unreachable` instruction executed
#
# wasmtime exited 134 on each p3panic run.

# Committed readings for p3bind.wasm, F2's witness (#1237): a GOOS=wasip3 guest calling the fork's generated
# WASI 0.3 bindings directly.
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run p3bind.wasm f2-bindings-token   (from this directory)
#              burroughs run p3bind.wasm -- f2-bindings-token
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64, 2026-11-15.
#
# reader                                      reading
# p3bind argv                                 p3bind.wasm f2-bindings-token
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3bind, 10 of 10         exit 127
# Burroughs at 3175bdb on p3bind, 10 of 10    exit 127
#
# Meaning:     the exit code is the bitmask of properties p3bind observed (p3bind.go.txt), reported through
#              wasi:cli/exit@0.3.0's exit-with-code because F2 lowers no stdout. 127 is all seven:
#                0x01 argv[1] is the token            (get-arguments: a list<string> through cabi_realloc)
#                0x02 monotonic now does not decrease  (one flat i64 result, twice)
#                0x04 system-clock now's nanoseconds < 1e9   (the u32 at offset 8 of a 16-byte return area)
#                0x08 its seconds > 1.6e9                    (the s64 at offset 0)
#                0x10 get-random-bytes(16): 1 to 16 bytes, not all zero  (a list<u8> through cabi_realloc)
#                0x20 two get-random-u64 results differ
#                0x40 two get-insecure-seed results are equal  (a tuple<u64, u64> through a return area)
#              Neither engine wrote a byte to stdout or stderr. The argv is the same on both: wasmtime passes
#              the path it was given, and burroughs run passes the file's base name, so run from this
#              directory both are "p3bind.wasm".
#
# The neuters, each run 10 of 10 on both engines, with the builds kept out of this tree:
#   argv[1] "f2-bindings-wrong":                    wasmtime exit 126, Burroughs exit 126 (0x01 clears).
#   instant.nanoseconds read at offset 0, not 8     wasmtime exit 123, Burroughs exit 123 (0x04 clears:
#   (the generated system_clock.go edited):         the low word of the seconds is above 1e9).
#   the linker's trap stand-in forced, internal/wasi's cabi_realloc left unexported: wasmtime exit 134,
#   Burroughs `trap: unreachable` exit 4. wasmtime's stderr, identical in all 10 runs, names the stand-in:
#
#     Error: failed to run main module `p3bind-fallback.wasm`
#
#     Caused by:
#         0: error while executing at wasm backtrace:
#         0: 0x117644 - <unknown>!cabi_realloc
#         1: 0x1c1669 - wit-component:shim!indirect-wasi:cli/environment@0.3.0-get-arguments
#         2: 0x115fb2 - <unknown>!internal_wasi_generated_cli.wasmimportGetArguments
#         3: 0x1163c6 - <unknown>!internal_wasi_generated_cli.GetArguments
#         4: 0x117233 - <unknown>!main.main
#         5:  0x9707c - <unknown>!runtime.main
#         6: 0x113c2b - <unknown>!wasm_pc_f_loop
#         7: 0x113d3e - <unknown>!_rt0_wasm_wasip3
#         1: wasm trap: wasm `unreachable` instruction executed
